<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamprin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamprin is a small municipality in the Unterland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschnerberg">Eschnerberg</a>, comprising the villages of Gamprin and Bendern. On the church hill at Bendern the people of the Lordship of Schellenberg swore allegiance to Prince Hans-Adam I of Liechtenstein in 1699. The lordship later became the Unterland of the Principality of Liechtenstein.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamprin.jpg" alt="Gamprin" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markus K. Mayer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirchh%C3%BCgel_Bendern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gamprin is a municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, on the border with the Swiss municipality of Sennwald. It lies in the hilly landscape west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschnerberg">Eschnerberg</a> and consists of the villages of Gamprin and Bendern, in a scattered settlement that includes the old hamlets of Au, Bühl, Badäl, Salums and Gölla. It covers 6.188 square kilometres. It also has an exclave on the Nendler Berg, and its Gampriner Seele, a lake of 2.6 hectares, is the only lake in Liechtenstein. A flood of the Rhine in 1927 created it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement here goes back to the Stone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Ages</a>, and the archaeological site of Lutzengüetle lies within the parish. The name comes from the Old Romansh camp Rin, a field on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It was first mentioned about 1150 as Camporin, and in 1253 as Gamperin. The Bendern Parish Church, dedicated to Mary, has a nave dating from the 8th or 9th century, enlarged in the 14th century, and a Gothic chancel inaugurated in 1481. In 1499, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-war">Swabian War</a>, Swiss Confederates pillaged the village. The oldest surviving village charter, of 1643, describes the villagers' rights and duties in using pastures and forests, and in 1699 the men of the Unterland swore allegiance to the Prince at Bendern.</p><p>In the 18th and 19th centuries Bendern ran one of the five ferries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a> stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, linking it with Haag on the opposite bank. A wooden bridge was built across the river in 1867–68.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Until 1950 Gamprin had neither a coat of arms nor an official flag. A committee designed them, and the municipality adopted them in 1958. The yellow ribbon stands for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and the roses come from the arms of the knight Rüdiger von Limpach, who owned property in Gamprin in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The municipality contains the Liechtenstein Institute and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lgt-group">LGT Group</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gamprin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Schellenberg was a rural district of a few villages, including Eschen, Mauren and <b>Gamprin</b>, ruled by distant lords through a bailiff.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-schellenberg">Lordship of Schellenberg</a></li></ul></section>
